QR Forge is built with privacy as a core principle. This policy explains what data the extension handles, where it stays, and what it never does.

The short version

QR Forge runs entirely in your browser.
Your saved QR codes, history, and favorites are stored locally on your device.
Nothing is sent to any server. There is no backend.
No tracking, no analytics, no ads, no accounts.


What QR Forge stores locally
The extension uses Chrome's local storage to save:

Your QR history — saved only when you click "Save to History." Includes the URLs, text, Wi-Fi credentials, contact details, or other content you've encoded into QR codes, along with their visual settings (colors, margin, error correction level, logo overlay).
Your favorites — the same data for any QR code you've explicitly starred.
Your preferences — UI state such as whether you've dismissed the onboarding tip.

This data lives only on your device, in your browser's local storage. It is not synced, transmitted, or accessible to anyone but you. If you uninstall the extension, all of it is permanently deleted.
QR codes you generate but do not save are never written to storage.

What QR Forge does NOT do

❌ No data is sent to QR Forge, its developer, or any third party
❌ No analytics or usage tracking
❌ No advertising, no ad networks
❌ No user accounts or sign-in
❌ No automatic collection — content is only saved when you explicitly click "Save to History" or "★" (Save to Favorites)
❌ No collection of personal information, browsing history, or website content
❌ No selling, sharing, or transfer of your data to anyone


What permissions are used and why

storage — to save your QR history, favorites, and preferences locally on your device
clipboardWrite — to copy generated QR images and favorite content to your clipboard when you click a Copy button
activeTab / tabs — to read the current tab's URL only when you explicitly click "Use Current Tab"
contextMenus — to add the QR Forge right-click menu (Generate QR for page, Generate QR for selection, Scan QR in image, Copy saved favorites)
scripting — to write to the clipboard when you copy a favorite from the right-click menu
notifications — to confirm clipboard actions visually and notify when a right-click action requires opening the popup
offscreen — as a clipboard fallback on pages where script injection isn't allowed (e.g., chrome:// pages)
<all_urls> (host permission) — required for the right-click context menu to function on any page you visit. The extension does not read, collect, or transmit page content.


QR code scanning
When you decode a QR code — by dragging an image into the Scan tab or right-clicking a QR image on a webpage — the image is processed entirely within your browser using a local JavaScript library. The image data and the decoded result are never uploaded anywhere.

Logos and uploaded images
If you upload a logo image to embed in a QR code, it is read locally by your browser. It is only saved to local storage if you explicitly save the QR to History or Favorites. The logo never leaves your device.

Children's privacy
QR Forge does not knowingly collect any data from anyone, including children. There is no data collection at all.

Changes to this policy
If a future version of QR Forge ever changes how it handles data, this policy will be updated and the version date above will reflect the change. Material changes will also be noted in the extension's release notes.

